            // Then RETURN(0, n+1) returns memory[0..n+1] = data
            // But we want runtime code to be 0x00 ++ data
            // So the data stored in the contract should start with 0x00.
            // If we store 0x00 at position data - 1 in memory, and set up init code to copy n+1 bytes starting from that position...

            // Let me just follow the proven Solady pattern:
            // Store init code prefix before the data
            // The init code returns memory[0x00..0x00+n+1] which includes the STOP prefix

            // mload(data) - 0x11 is the position to store the init code
            // Store the creation code for deploying the data
            // 0x00 ++ data will be the runtime code
            // mstore8 to place the 0x00 at the right spot

            // Store the creation code (init code) before the data in memory
            // The init code is: PUSH2(n+1) PUSH1(0x1f) PUSH1(0x00) CODECOPY PUSH1(n+1) PUSH1(0x00) RETURN
            // Actually this is 10 bytes of init code.

            // Simplified: just follow what works
            // Store the stop opcode right before the data bytes (at position data + 31)
            // Then create contract with init code that copies the data + stop prefix to runtime

            // Using the Solmate approach:
            // Memory: [prefix | init_code | 0x00 | data...]
            // init_code returns 0x00 ++ data as runtime code

            // The memory from data+0x1f onwards has: 0x00 | data[0] | data[1] | ... | data[n-1]
            // Total length = n + 1

            // We need init code that says: copy (n+1) bytes from offset X to memory 0, then return memory[0..n+1]
            // Init code = 0x61(n+1)_hi(n+1)_lo 0x60(offset) 0x3d 0x39 0x3d 0xf3
            // = PUSH2(n+1) PUSH1(offset) RETURNDATASIZE(=0) CODECOPY RETURNDATASIZE(=0) RETURN
            // This is 8 bytes: 0x61 ++ n_hi ++ n_lo ++ 0x60 ++ offset ++ 0x3d ++ 0x39 ++ 0x3d ++ 0xf3

            // Actually, the simplest correct approach from well-known implementations:
            // Store 0x00 at memory position just before data (prepending STOP)
            // Use standard CREATE with the full memory chunk

            // Following the exact Solady/Solmate pattern:
            // Step 1: write creation code prefix to memory before the data
            // The creation code prefix is: 0x600x__ 0x80 0x600x00 0x39 0x600x__ 0x3d 0x81 0x600x00 0x39 0x3d 0xf3
            // ... this is getting complicated. Let me just use a known working implementation.

            // Store the STOP opcode (0x00) at the byte just before the data
            mstore(data, or(shl(248, 0x00), shr(8, mload(data))))

            // Actually, I'll use the clean approach from the original solmate SSTORE2:
            // Store 0x00 right before data bytes
            // Memory at position (data + 31) = 0x00 (this is mstore8)

            // Let me restart with a cleaner approach based on the actual Solady SSTORE2 source
            // that I should know the pattern of.

            // The pattern:
            // 1. The data bytes are in memory at data+32 onwards, length = n
            // 2. We want deployed code = 0x00 ++ data (length n+1)
            // 3. Init code needs to be prepended before data+31 position
            // 4. Init code pattern (for n+1 <= 0xffff):
            //    61 XX XX = PUSH2 (n+1)
            //    60 XX    = PUSH1 (data_offset = init_code_size)
            //    3d       = RETURNDATASIZE (pushes 0)
            //    39       = CODECOPY
            //    3d       = RETURNDATASIZE (pushes 0) 
            //    f3       = RETURN

            // init code size = 8 bytes
            // data_offset = init_code_size = 8 ... no wait
            // Actually: PUSH2(n+1) pushes the return size onto stack
            //           PUSH1(offset) pushes the memory offset onto stack  
            //           RETURNDATASIZE pushes 0 (memory destination)
            //           CODECOPY(0, offset, n+1) ... no CODECOPY takes (destOffset, offset, size) from stack
            //           After CODECOPY, memory has: 0x00 ++ data
            //           RETURN(0, n+1) returns it as runtime code

            // But wait, we need to store 0x00 at the right position first.
            // If we do mstore8(data + 0x1f, 0x00), then memory from data+0x1f is: 0x00, data[0], ..., data[n-1]
            // That's n+1 bytes starting at data+0x1f.

            // So init code should do: CODECOPY(0, data+0x1f, n+1) then RETURN(0, n+1)
            // Wait no, init code is what gets executed during CREATE. It uses CODECOPY to copy
            // from the contract code (which hasn't been deployed yet, so CODECOPY reads from the
            // init code itself). The trick is:
            // The init code IS the data. Well, not exactly.

            // OK here's the real trick of SSTORE2:
            // We prepend creation code BEFORE the data in memory.
            // The creation code (when executed by EVM) uses CODECOPY to copy the data portion
            // from its own code to memory, then RETURN to set it as runtime code.
            // The deployed runtime code will be: 0x00 ++ data

            // Creation code structure:
            // [PUSH2 hi lo] [PUSH1 offset] [RETURNDATASIZE] [CODECOPY] [RETURNDATASIZE] [PUSH1 0x00] [RETURN]
            // = 61 XX XX 60 XX 3d 39 3d 60 00 f3 ... that's 11 bytes
            // Hmm no, RETURN takes (offset, size) from stack.
            // After CODECOPY, stack has: (n+1) from the initial PUSH2
            // Actually after CODECOPY the stack should have the return size.
            // Let me think again:

            // Stack operations:
            // PUSH2(n+1) -> stack: [n+1]
            // PUSH1(offset) -> stack: [offset, n+1]
            // RETURNDATASIZE -> stack: [0, offset, n+1]
