            // PUSH2(n+1) -> [n+1, 0]... wait that puts n+1 on top

            // Let me reconsider:
            // PUSH2(n+1) -> [n+1]
            // DUP1 -> [n+1, n+1]
            // PUSH1(offset) -> [offset, n+1, n+1]
            // RETURNDATASIZE -> [0, offset, n+1, n+1]
            // CODECOPY -> memory[0..n+1] = code[offset..offset+n+1], stack: [n+1]
            // RETURNDATASIZE -> [0, n+1]
            // RETURN -> returns memory[0..n+1]
            // Total: 12 bytes. That's too many.

            // Actually the standard pattern from known implementations:
